The element-token system has as neighbourhoods the brackets of neighbourhoods of the original system, and its master is the set of all elements. That set is nonempty because it contains the bottom element, and it is a neighbourhood because it equals the bracket of the master. If the bracket of a neighbourhood W is contained in the intersection of the brackets of neighbourhoods X and Y, the principal filter of W lies in the bracket of W and hence in both brackets, and the second components of those memberships are the inclusions of W in X and in Y. The intersection law makes X ∩ Y a neighbourhood, and the intersection of the two brackets equals its bracket. Every neighbourhood is contained in the set of all elements.
